# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The entire edge routing table. Caddy auto-provisions Let's Encrypt certs
# per hostname (HTTP-01), so every site below gets HTTPS with no extra config.
#
# DOMAIN and ACME_EMAIL are read from the .env file (see docker-compose.yml),
# so this file is reusable and the domain is never hard-coded.
#
# Adding a project = paste the 3-line template block at the bottom, change the
# subdomain + container name, and `caddy reload`. The wildcard DNS A record
# means no DNS change is needed for a new subdomain.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# ── Shared: Digital Asset Links ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Android app (a Trusted Web Activity — see android/) is only allowed to
# show a site full-screen, with no browser URL bar, if that site's ORIGIN serves
# /.well-known/assetlinks.json naming the app's package and signing fingerprint.
#
# Every project subdomain is a separate origin, so every one of them needs that
# file — but the projects live in their own repos and none of them should have
# to know the app exists. So the edge answers for them: this snippet serves the
# platform's single copy from /srv/site, and everything else still reaches the
# project container untouched.
#
# `handle` sorts before `reverse_proxy` in Caddy's directive order, so a request
# for any OTHER path falls straight through to the proxy below it — which is why
# each project block stays bare. Verified end to end: the upstream still sees the
# original path and Host, and bustracker's header block still applies to both.
#
# The apex needs no import — it already serves /srv/site with file_server, and
# Caddy's file_server does serve dot-directories.
(assetlinks) {
	handle /.well-known/assetlinks.json {
		root * /srv/site
		file_server
	}
}

# ── Shared: HSTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tells browsers to use HTTPS for this host from now on, even when someone types
# http:// or follows an old link, so a network attacker can't hold a visitor on
# plain HTTP (security fix plan 5.11). Every site block imports it, the apex and
# www included; CI fails the build if one doesn't.
#
# It is the one header the edge owns for EVERY site: no app sets it, so it
# doesn't count against the "keep this block bare" rule below. The `>` defers it
# to when the response is written, so it replaces an upstream's value rather
# than doubling it (the www redirect gets it too).
#
# Deliberately cautious: one day, no includeSubDomains, no preload (preload is
# near-permanent and covers every subdomain, present and future). Raise it to a
# year (31536000) once it has been live for a week with every site fine.
(hsts) {
	header >Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=86400"
	# Responses Caddy writes as ERRORS (the private apps' 401, a 502 while an
	# app restarts) skip the header above, so the error route sets it too. A
	# site that ever needs its own handle_errors must repeat this line there.
	handle_errors {
		header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=86400"
	}
}

# ── Shared: edge auth for PRIVATE apps ───────────────────────────────────────
# A project meant for me only adds `import private <user> <bcrypt hash>` to its
# block; public projects simply don't. Browsers cache Basic credentials per
# subdomain, so each private app asks once per device, then never again.
#
# EACH app has its own credential, and none lives in this (public) repo: the
# blocks below pass `{$EDGE_AUTH_<APP>_USER}` / `{$EDGE_AUTH_<APP>_HASH}` from
# the server's .env (see .env.example; SETUP.md "Edge passwords" rotates them).
# Until 2026-09-30 one credential, hash committed here, guarded every private app
# and the LiftMap Dashboard (security fix plan 5.3).
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: when a variable is missing, the default is user `locked` with
# the bcrypt of a random secret that was thrown away the moment it was hashed —
# so Caddy still starts, every other site stays up, and that one app refuses
# every password until its credential is set. Never replace the default with a
# real credential.
#
# The browser sends the credential with every request after the first; the
# apps have no use for it, so `request_header` drops it here and it never
# reaches a container (basic_auth checks it first: it sorts before
# request_header in Caddy's directive order).
#
# /.well-known/assetlinks.json is excluded on purpose: Chrome fetches it with
# NO credentials when the Android TWA verifies the origin, so putting it behind
# auth would silently break full-screen mode for every private app. basic_auth
# sorts BEFORE handle in Caddy's directive order, which is why the exclusion
# must live here as a matcher rather than relying on the assetlinks handle.
(private) {
	@needs_auth not path /.well-known/assetlinks.json
	basic_auth @needs_auth {
		{args[0]} {args[1]}
	}
	request_header -Authorization
}

# ── Apex: the homepage hub, served straight off disk (no homepage container) ──
{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	root * /srv/site
	file_server
	encode zstd gzip # homepage is static; safe to compress here
}

# www → apex (single canonical homepage)
www.{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	redir https://{$DOMAIN}{uri} permanent
}

# ── Project #1: roadtrip — reached by container name over its edge network ──
# Keep this block BARE apart from the shared imports: roadtrip's Flask already
# gzips (Flask-Compress) and sets X-Content-Type-Options / X-Frame-Options /
# Referrer-Policy. Adding `encode` or headers here would double-compress and
# emit doubled headers. Caddy passes the app's responses straight through.
roadtrip.{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy roadtrip:8000
}

# ── Project #2: availability — personal availability calendar ────────────────
# Keep this block BARE apart from the shared imports: the Flask app already
# gzips (Flask-Compress) and sets X-Content-Type-Options / X-Frame-Options /
# Referrer-Policy (and no-store on /api/*). Adding `encode` or headers here
# would double them up.
availability.{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy availability:8000
}

# ── Project #3: bustracker — live Tbilisi bus map ────────────────────────────
# NOT bare, and NOT `encode` either. This one is a stdlib http.server, not Flask:
# server.py gzips its own responses (anything over 1 KB, with Content-Encoding +
# Vary), so adding `encode` here would double-compress — but it sets none of the
# security headers the Flask apps set, so those go on at the edge.
# It listens on 8765, not 8000.
bustracker.{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	}
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy bustracker:8765
}

# ── Project #4: citywatch — public live camera feeds ─────────────────────────
# Keep this block BARE apart from the shared imports: the container is nginx
# serving a static Vite build, and its own config already gzips (gzip on, over
# 1 KB, with Vary) and sets Content-Security-Policy / X-Content-Type-Options /
# Referrer-Policy on every location. Adding `encode` or headers here would
# double them up.
#
# It sets no X-Frame-Options on purpose — its CSP carries `frame-ancestors
# 'self'`, which supersedes it. Don't "fix" that by adding the header here.
#
# It listens on 80 (nginx default), not 8000.
citywatch.{$DOMAIN} {
	import hsts
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy citywatch:80
}

# ── Project #5: flights — airport-pickup flight tracker ──────────────────────
# NOT bare, and NOT `encode` either. Like bustracker this is a stdlib
# http.server: server.py gzips its own responses (over 1 KB, with
# Content-Encoding + Vary), so adding `encode` here would double-compress —
# but it sets none of the security headers the Flask apps set, so those go on
# at the edge. It listens on 8766, not 8000.
#
# PRIVATE: personal app — `import private` puts the whole subdomain behind the
# edge basic_auth, with its own credential (assetlinks excluded inside the snippet).
flights.{$DOMAIN} {
	import private {$EDGE_AUTH_FLIGHTS_USER:locked} {$EDGE_AUTH_FLIGHTS_HASH:$2a$14$9MZGjXmdE909qdYVvzqwA.uVEz2MgWIioYtPnUFe3A932J8L0ObL2}
	import hsts
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	}
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy flights:8766
}

# ── Project #6: speed — GPS speed + average-speed recorder ───────────────────
# Keep this block BARE apart from the imports: the container is nginx serving a
# static PWA, and its own config already gzips (over 1 KB, with Vary) and sets
# X-Content-Type-Options / X-Frame-Options / Referrer-Policy on every response.
# Adding `encode` or headers here would double them up.
#
# nginx also owns the .webmanifest content type over there. nginx's mime.types
# has no entry for it, and Chrome will not treat the app as installable unless
# it arrives as application/manifest+json — so that override lives with the app,
# not here.
#
# It listens on 8767, not 8000.
#
# PRIVATE: personal app — `import private` puts the whole subdomain behind the
# edge basic_auth, with its own credential (assetlinks excluded inside the snippet).
speed.{$DOMAIN} {
	import private {$EDGE_AUTH_SPEED_USER:locked} {$EDGE_AUTH_SPEED_HASH:$2a$14$9MZGjXmdE909qdYVvzqwA.uVEz2MgWIioYtPnUFe3A932J8L0ObL2}
	import hsts
	import assetlinks
	reverse_proxy speed:8767
}

# ── Project #7: oktopus — gym class picker + auto-booker ─────────────────────
# NOT bare. Like bustracker and flights this is a stdlib http.server, so it sets
# none of the security headers the Flask apps set and those go on here. UNLIKE
# those two it does NOT gzip itself, so `encode` belongs at the edge — adding it
# is correct here and would be double-compression over there. Don't copy this
# block's `encode` line into theirs.
#
# The upstream name is `oktopus-booker`, NOT `oktopus`: the container is the
# booking WORKER that predates this subdomain, and the picker is a thread inside
# it (it shares the booker's cached JWT rather than logging in twice). Caddy
# routes by container name, so the mismatch with the subdomain is deliberate.
#
# It listens on 8768, not 8000.
#
# PRIVATE: personal app, and every click books a class on my membership —
# `import private` puts the whole subdomain behind its own edge credential.
oktopus.{$DOMAIN} {
	import private {$EDGE_AUTH_OKTOPUS_USER:locked} {$EDGE_AUTH_OKTOPUS_HASH:$2a$14$9MZGjXmdE909qdYVvzqwA.uVEz2MgWIioYtPnUFe3A932J8L0ObL2}
	import hsts
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	}
	import assetlinks
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy oktopus-booker:8768
}

# ── Template for the next project (copy, change the two names, uncomment) ─────
# The `import hsts` and `import assetlinks` lines are part of the template (CI
# checks both): without the second that project opens with a URL bar inside the
# Android app. See CONTRACT.md.
# newproj.{$DOMAIN} {
#     import hsts
#     import assetlinks
#     reverse_proxy newproj:8000
